  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change the color of a `<span>` on mouse over. Here they are, from simplest to most flexible:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: Pure CSS `:hover` (Recommended)\n\nIf you can identify an element by ID (`#`) or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make the changes only appear when the user has moused over an element.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nYou can also target a specific `<span>` using a class or ID:\n\n```css\n/* By class */\n.my-span:hover {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\n/* By ID */\n#my-span:hover {\n  color: green;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript (`onmouseover` / `onmouseout`)\n\nThe JavaScript code must be attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`, which replace the CSS selector `:hover`.\n\nInside those quotes, translating CSS to JavaScript requires these conversions: use the `this` keyword to refer to the element, add the `.style.` property to show you are changing its style, and convert hyphenated CSS attributes (like `color`) to camelCase JavaScript ones.\n\n```html\n<span\n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n>\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript (`addEventListener`)\n\nAdding JavaScript inside HTML tags (inline) is inflexible and time-consuming. Fortunately, you can change styles or classes of HTML elements in JavaScript at the top of your page or in a separate file.\n\nThe `onmouseover` event is triggered when the user hovers over an element with the mouse.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      #my-span {\n        color: black;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"my-span\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"my-span\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\"; // revert color on mouse out\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Which method should you use?\n\n| Method | Best For |\n|---|---|\n| **CSS `:hover`** | Simple, clean styling \u2014 always preferred |\n| **Inline JS** | Quick tests or very simple cases |\n| **External JS** | Dynamic or complex color-change logic |\n\nThe **CSS `:hover` method** is almost always the best choice as it keeps your styling clean, separated, and easy to maintain.",
